This repository retains Goatr patches/locks/source metadata and the original
MIT/Apache-2.0 license texts for source-pinned iroh-ffi. Nix fetches the pinned
upstream tree, applies those patches and generates the complete Kotlin API and
two Android native outputs from its independently retained lock. The original
Android context helper is copied unchanged into the Nix output. See
third_party/iroh-ffi/NOTICE and source.json for revisions, changes and terms.

The companion uses direct iroh, not host FFI. Independently retained Cargo
locks and dependencies.json files record exact registry identities, resolved
features and declared licenses. Product Nix outputs include dependency
LICENSE/NOTICE/COPYING files from locked source archives; Goatr's GPL does not
relicense the original third-party code. JNA5.19.1 is separately resolved as
an Android AAR and a JVM-only JAR (LGPL-2.1-or-later OR Apache-2.0).

Writable local configuration uses toml_edit0.25.15+spec-1.1.0's display/serde
serializer and toml_writer1.1.2+spec-1.1.0 (MIT OR Apache-2.0), Copyright (c)
Individual contributors. Exact registry archive SHA256 and resolved features
are retained in companion/Cargo.lock and dependencies.json; Nix's existing
locked-source license collector includes both upstream LICENSE-MIT and
LICENSE-APACHE. toml_writer's crate source records revision
eb251609a333580bf005cae27df853bb63dffdbe, crates/toml_writer; this is publisher
source metadata, not an independently reproduced source/archive identity.

Read-only host Git review uses the same locked nixpkgs Git derivation in the
product and development shells, not a Cargo library or a system-PATH fallback.
The generated goatrManifest.readGit retains its actual source/patches/build
options, direct ELF/interpreter identities and exact runtime NAR/reference
graph. Git's actual source COPYING and the runtime outputs' packaged notice
files are retained in share/licenses/goatr/read-git. The locked Nixpkgs recipe
tree and derivation preserve separate non-Cargo source/license provenance;
the Cargo notice collector does not establish coverage of that closure.
Native runtime compatibility and distribution/compliance approval remain
separate from those source records. This does not change installed native tools.

The product UnifiedPush publisher reuses exact reqwest0.13.5 through HTTP2
and Rustls with no automatic retry/proxy/redirect; httpdate1.0.3 parses bounded
Retry-After dates. Non-installed owned camera acceptance also reuses reqwest
and http-body-util0.1.5 (MIT, Copyright (c) 2019-2026 Sean McArthur & Hyper
Contributors). The resolved acceptance graph
adds h2 0.4.19 (MIT, Copyright (c) 2017 h2 authors) and fnv1.0.7 (MIT OR
Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2017 Contributors). Exact archive checksums/features
and selected dependency declarations are retained in companion/Cargo.lock and
dependencies.json; source archives contain the complete terms retained by the
existing Nix license collector. The HTTP client is now a product dependency; this does not claim actual
push delivery or Android compatibility. Android connector dependencies have
independent Gradle locks/checksums and app notice assets. The fixed native controller protobuf and
grayscale sealed-memfd PNG consumer are independent code, not copied SDK/qemu
implementation; Android SDK license acceptance remains project-scoped.

Non-installed stock-control acceptance uses the existing retained ring0.17.14
(Apache-2.0 AND ISC) as an exact dev-only direct dependency for standard
P-256/SHA256/ASN1 signature verification; archive SHA256
a4689e6c2294d81e88dc6261c768b63bc4fcdb852be6d1352498b114f61383b7.
Its existing locked source/complete license terms remain retained by the Nix
collector; no new external package or Android crypto dependency is introduced.
The test-only Android lifecycle runner uses platform EC/SHA256withECDSA with an
ephemeral in-memory key, not copied crypto or a persistent AndroidKeyStore alias.

Gradle wrapper scripts/JAR are generated by Gradle9.8.0, Apache-2.0;
gradle/wrapper/source.json records their actual identities. The Android SDK
remains subject to Google's license, accepted explicitly for this project's
optional Android shells/native builds, not global host configuration.

No AgentWire, Herdr, Codex or private deployment implementation is copied
here. Do not reuse AgentWire code before its full notice is available.

